**CONFIRMED: sandbox bypass.** A planned write through an in-base symlinked parent passes `resolveWithinAllowedBase` (lexical fallback) and the file **materializes outside the base** at the symlink target. This is exactly what the PR claims to prevent ("an in-base link pointing outside is rejected too" — only true when the full path exists).

This is a 🚨 BLOCKING security finding. Per the verdict mapping: security issue → recommendation `BLOCK`.

The allowed_base_test.dart has a test 'blocks an in-base symlink pointing outside' — but it tests `resolveWithinAllowedBase('escape', base.path)` where the symlink IS the final component (exists → resolved). The gap is symlinked ANCESTORS of a not-yet-existing target.

Both findings (macOS false rejection + symlink escape) share one root cause and one fix: canonicalize the nearest existing ancestor of the path and append the remaining (non-existent) tail, e.g.:

```dart
String _canonicalizePath(String path) {
  var current = path;
  final trail = <String>[];
  while (true) {
    try {
      return '${Directory(current).resolveSymbolicLinksSync()}'
          '${trail.isEmpty ? '' : '/${trail.reversed.join('/')}'}';
    } catch (_) {
      final parent = p.dirname(current);
      if (parent == current) return path; // reached root, nothing exists
      trail.add(p.basename(current));
      current = parent;
    }
  }
```

